course: dictionary
lesson: 14835
title: Radial
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Radial means spreading out from a centre, like the spokes of a wheel or the rays of the sun. «The spokes of the bike wheel make a radial pattern.» Picture a point in the middle with lines reaching outward from it.
explain: In maths, radial is linked to the radius of a circle, which is the distance from the centre to the edge. In science and PE, the radial bone is in your forearm, and you can feel your radial pulse at your wrist.
explain: You will also see radial in everyday words. «The roads run out from the town centre in radial lines.» A radial tyre is a kind of car tyre. To check a meaning, swap in «spreading out from a centre» and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Radial means spreading out from a centre.
fact: The radius of a circle runs from its centre to its edge, and radial is linked to it.
fact: «Radial symmetry» means the parts are arranged evenly around a centre, as in a starfish or a flower.
fact: The radial bone is in your forearm, and your radial pulse is felt at your wrist.
fact: A «radial road» is a road leading out from a city centre, and a «radial tyre» is a kind of car tyre.
exercise: «The spokes of the wheel make a radial pattern.» What does radial mean here? | Made of metal | Round and flat like a coin | *Spreading out from a centre | Very fast
exercise: In maths, radial is linked to the radius of which shape? | A square | A straight line | *A circle | A triangle
exercise: Where would you feel your radial pulse? | On top of your head | *At your wrist | At your knee | In your ear
test: «Starfish have radial symmetry.» What does radial symmetry mean? | No shape at all | *Parts spread out evenly from a centre | Only one side | Two halves that are mirror images
test: Which of these is a radial road? | A quiet lane through the woods | A road that runs under a river | A gravel track on a farm | *A road leading out from a city centre
exam: Which word best describes a pattern like the rays of the sun? | Sticky | Vertical | Hollow | *Radial
